{
  "content": "<updated_cheatsheet>\n### Sequencing: how to analyze evolving conversations\n- Start by building a **running constraint set from user messages only**. After every new user turn, re-evaluate the entire task spec rather than appending to an old conclusion.\n- Treat later user turns as **highest-priority revisions** to the spec, not just extra details. If a new shard changes totals, categories, or state transitions, invalidate earlier inferences that depended on the older interpretation.\n- Wait to finalize task understanding until all user-stated constraints are collected. A conversation may look underspecified early on but become fully solvable once later shards arrive.\n- When the user introduces an aggregate, total, remainder, or final leftover after earlier partial amounts, **reconstruct the current state before reasoning backward**. Many failures come from confusing an intermediate value with the final state.\n- If the latest user message completes a missing partition or relation, switch from \u201cneeds clarification\u201d thinking to full system evaluation. Do not keep stale uncertainty after the spec becomes complete.\n\n### State tracking: what to compare across turns\n- Track quantities as a **state-transition chain**: identify what is initial, what is intermediate, and what is current at each turn. This is especially important when the same object changes state multiple times.\n- Check whether each new disclosure refers to the **same entity in a later state** or to a different subset/category. The analyzer should propagate the state change through the spec instead of treating each mention independently.\n- When later turns reveal a remaining amount plus an amount already kept/used/set aside, combine those into the total at that stage before solving any earlier unknowns.\n- For staged percentages, costs, or quantities, verify whether each value applies to the **original total** or to a **remainder after prior deductions**. Misreading the base is a common source of false alignment.\n- After a new shard arrives, compare the assistant\u2019s response against the **full accumulated spec**, not just the latest message. A mathematically valid intermediate result can still be wrong as the final answer.\n\n### Diagnostic patterns: what signals an error\n- Flag any assistant inference that fills a gap the user has not actually filled. This is especially important when the answer depends on a category split, remainder, hidden total, or other unstated partition.\n- If the assistant proposes a value using an unsupported assumption, mark it as wrong if later user turns make that assumption unnecessary, incomplete, or false.\n- Distinguish between **derived sub-results** and the actual requested target. A correct intermediate fact is not aligned if the user asked for a different quantity, final state, or output variable.\n- Separate arithmetic correctness from specification alignment. The assistant may compute correctly from partial data while still failing the user\u2019s final intent.\n- If the assistant asks for more information, check whether later user turns already supply that information implicitly or explicitly. Earlier \u201cneed clarification\u201d reasoning becomes obsolete once the conversation resolves it.\n- When the user adds a total/sum constraint after earlier relational clues, this usually means the analyzer should look for a **system-wide completion**, not continue treating the problem as underdetermined.\n\n### What to preserve vs. what to discard\n- In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d keep only calculations and relationships that remain valid under the **full accumulated user constraints**. Discard partial conclusions that depended on an incomplete terminal condition.\n- In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d tie the flaw to the exact user evidence that was missing at the time. The corrective note should make clear why the assistant\u2019s step was unjustified under the full conversation.\n- When new information resolves an earlier uncertainty, mark prior \u201cneeds clarification\u201d reasoning as obsolete and note the anchoring risk.\n- If the assistant has already gone down speculative branches, compact the context by removing obsolete uncertainty and irrelevant assumptions so later analysis stays aligned with the actual specification.\n\n### Common pitfalls to watch for\n- Do not let earlier assistant reasoning anchor the analysis summary. If it was based on partial data, it should not survive unchanged once the user adds a decisive constraint.\n- Do not keep stale uncertainty after the user has completed the specification. A later shard can turn an incomplete problem into a fully determined one.\n- Watch for assistant-added assumptions that were never in the user messages, especially extra categories, fees, charge types, alternative formulas, or other invented structure.\n- Do not treat user repetition as noise. Repeated or incremental user messages often refine the task, resolve ambiguity, or supply the missing piece needed to solve it.\n- Avoid overcomplicating the task with an unnecessary template when the accumulated user constraints support a simpler consistent interpretation.\n- Do not focus on whether the assistant used a generally valid method; focus on whether it matches the **evolving user intent** and the final accumulated specification.\n\n### Response-shaping priorities for the analyzer\n- The final judgment should reflect the **current task state after all user shards**, not the state visible at any earlier turn.\n- The analyzer should explicitly note when a later user message changes the implied total, base, or target variable, because that is often the exact point where alignment breaks.\n- When the conversation history contains enough information to determine the answer, the analyzer should expect a final result rather than more questions.\n- For context-edit or compacted summaries, preserve validated relationships, the latest decisive constraint, and the actual target; remove obsolete totals, stale assumptions, and intermediate conclusions that no longer hold.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
